  "text": "**Solution 2 is wrong.** Its \"19th term\" is the same 36-digit number Solutions 1 and 3 give for n = 18, so it reports a(18) as the answer to n = 19.\n\n**Solutions 1 and 3 disagree on a(19).** Both agree on a(17) and a(18), and both give a(19) about 2.67×10³ times a(18), so the growth ratio doesn't separate them. I checked a congruence instead. Every consecutive pair of known values I could test satisfies a(n) ≡ a(n−2) (mod 3):\n\n- Small values: a(1)=1 ≡ a(3)=19 ≡ 1; a(2)=3 ≡ a(4)=219 ≡ 0; a(5)=4231 ≡ a(7)=6129859 ≡ 1; a(6)=130023 ≡ a(8)=431723379 ≡ 0; a(7) ≡ a(9)=44511042511 ≡ 1.\n- Larger values: a(15) and a(17) both have digit sum ≡ 1 (mod 3); a(16) and a(18) both have digit sum ≡ 0 (mod 3).\n\nSo a(19) should have digit sum ≡ 1 (mod 3), since a(17) ≡ 1.\n\n- **Solution 1:** 646022962653091240565034693557636043189 has digit sum 165 ≡ 0 (mod 3). It fails the pattern, and Solution 1 itself admits its checks don't reproduce the value.\n- **Solution 3:** 645631620258023552367430727161372339303 has digit sum 145 ≡ 1 (mod 3). It passes.\n\nThis is a heuristic check, not a proof, and I can't run code or look up OEIS A001035 to confirm the exact value. But it is the only evidence here that separates the two candidates, and it favours Solution 3.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
